# IsdaYou — Updated Build Checklist

The app functions in a hybrid offline/online mode:

- Offline (No Internet): Users can scan fish using the local YOLO model (TFLite) bundled inside the app, 
  view cached species details (FishSpecies), and save records locally (FishImage, FishIdentification).

- Online (With Internet): The app stream GPS updates (Location) to run geofence boundary checks (RestrictedArea.containsLocation()), 
  triggers seasonal breeding alerts (Notification), syncs pending logs, and executes automated web scrapers (GovernmentDataSource) 
  on the backend to pull live updates from Philippine fisheries portals (e.g., BFAR seasonal closed seasons in the Visayan Sea and Zamboanga Peninsula).

---

## 1. Set up GitHub (source code storage)
1.1. Create a GitHub account (if you don't have one) at github.com
1.2. Create a new repository, name it "isdayou"
1.3. Set it to Private
1.4. Add your teammates as collaborators (Settings → Collaborators → add by username or email)
1.5. Clone the repo locally (git clone [link])
1.6. Create three top-level folders: mobile-app/ (Android Studio project), backend/ (FastAPI + Web Scraper + PostGIS DB), and ml/ (YOLO training scripts & datasets)

## 2. Install your coding tools

For the mobile app (Android Client):
2.1. Download and install Android Studio (bundles Android SDK and emulator)
2.2. Install/verify JDK configuration inside Android Studio
2.3. Set up an emulator matching API 28 (Android 9.0+) and prepare physical budget Android devices for real-world camera and GPS testing

For the backend, web scrapers, and ML pipeline:
2.4. Download and install Git
2.5. Download and install Python 3.12 (used for FastAPI backend, scrapers, and Ultralytics training)
2.6. Download and install Node.js LTS (for the web-based admin console)
2.7. Download and install Docker Desktop (to run PostgreSQL with PostGIS extension)
2.8. Download and install VS Code with Python and ESLint extensions

## 3. Find and prepare your Philippine fish dataset
3.1. Identify target local species (e.g., Bangus, Galunggong, Tilapia, Mackerel, Sardines) prioritizing species with size limits or seasonal fishing bans
3.2. Source labeled Philippine fish images from Roboflow Universe, Kaggle, BFAR references, or local market photos (avoid capturing identifiable faces)
3.3. Organize images into structured directories by species name
3.4. Create a master CSV/JSON mapping for FishSpecies attributes: 
commonName, scientificName, localNames, description, habitat, averageSize, conservationStatus, minimumSize, and default fishingRestrictions

## 4. Train and export the AI engine (YOLOv11)
4.1. Install Ultralytics in Python: pip install ultralytics
4.2. Train the YOLOv11 object detection model on your organized Philippine fish dataset
4.3. Export the trained .pt weights to TensorFlow Lite (.tflite) for on-device execution: model.export(format="tflite")
4.4. Apply INT8 quantization to ensure fast inference and low memory footprint on budget phones
4.5. Generate and save a matching labels.txt class-index file alongside the TFLite model

## 5. Set up the backend database (PostgreSQL + PostGIS)
5.1. In backend/, configure a docker-compose.yml deploying PostgreSQL + PostGIS extension
5.2. Run docker compose up -d to spin up spatial database instances
5.3. Define database schemas mirroring your class diagram entities: User, FishImage, FishIdentification, FishSpecies, RestrictedArea, Location, Notification, and GovernmentDataSource
5.4. Verify connection using DBeaver

## 6. Build the backend & government web scraper (GovernmentDataSource)
6.1. In backend/, initialize virtual environment: python -m venv venv
6.2. Install packages: pip install fastapi uvicorn sqlalchemy psycopg2-binary beautifulsoup4 requests celery
6.3. Build Python web scrapers (GovernmentDataSource) targeting Philippine fisheries sites (BFAR, DA advisories) to automatically fetch seasonal breeding prohibitions, 
     date ranges (startDate, endDate), and restriction updates
6.4. Schedule automated background jobs (using APScheduler or Celery) running GovernmentDataSource.getUpdates() to auto-update RestrictedArea entries in PostGIS
6.5. Expose sync endpoints (/api/v1/sync/species, /api/v1/sync/geofences, /api/v1/notifications)

## 7. Build the mobile app (Android — Kotlin)
7.1. In Android Studio, initialize project in mobile-app/ (Empty Activity, Kotlin, API 28+)
7.2. Add dependencies in build.gradle:
   - CameraX (capturing/selecting FishImage)
   - TensorFlow Lite Android (org.tensorflow:tensorflow-lite)
   - Room DB (local offline cache for FishSpecies, FishIdentification, and queued logs)
   - Retrofit + OkHttp (online backend synchronization & alerts)
   - Play Services Location (Fused Location Provider) (reading device Location)
   - WorkManager (background log sync upon internet reconnection)
   - Jetpack Compose (UI implementation)
7.3. Place exported .tflite model and labels.txt inside app/src/main/assets/
7.4. Build CameraX integration to allow scanning or selecting an image from device gallery
7.5. Implement local model execution pipeline: image capture -> pre-processing -> TFLite inference -> display primarySpecies, confidence, and alternatives

## 8. Build the admin console (Web, admin only)
8.1. In backend/ or admin-console/, create Vite React project: npm create vite@latest . -- --template react
8.2. Build admin interface for fisheries personnel to manually add or adjust RestrictedArea geofences, seasonal breeding dates, 
     and regulatory mandates (FR-019 to FR-023)

## 9. Save your progress
9.1. git add .
9.2. git commit -m "Complete hybrid offline/online core architecture setup"
9.3. git push
9.4. Confirm your teammates can see the update on GitHub

## 10. Implement core features

Offline Features (No Internet Required):
10.1. Local Fish Identification: Run TFLite YOLO model on device $\rightarrow$ output primary match, confidence level, and alternative species possibilities
10.2. Offline Species Info: Query local Room DB (FishSpecies) to show scientific name, common name, local names, size limits, and cached rules
10.3. Local Identification Storage: Save offline scans locally into Room database

Online Features (Internet Connection Detected):
10.4. GPS Geofencing & Seasonal Alerts: Stream Location coordinates to backend/local engine -> run RestrictedArea.containsLocation() 
      and check if current date falls between startDate and endDate -> trigger Notification if user enters prohibited breeding/restricted zones
10.5. Data Synchronization: WorkManager uploads saved offline FishImage and FishIdentification logs to PostgreSQL server and 
      pulls updated FishSpecies & RestrictedArea rules scraped by GovernmentDataSource

## 11. Testing & Validation
11.1. AI Accuracy: Test scanning with unseen fish photos; verify confidence metrics and alternative rankings
11.2. Offline Integrity: Turn on Airplane Mode -> verify full camera scanning, TFLite inference, and local dataset lookup function smoothly
11.3. Geofencing & Alerts: Turn on Internet + Location -> test simulated coordinates inside a restricted breeding zone -> verify popup warning/notification triggers correctly
11.4. Web Scraper Test: Trigger GovernmentDataSource.getUpdates() script -> verify BFAR website updates correctly populate RestrictedArea table
11.5. Sync Queue: Run scans offline -> re-enable Wi-Fi/Mobile Data -> verify WorkManager syncs records without duplicate entries
11.6. Track all bugs in GitHub Issues tab

## 12. Refinement & Optimization
12.1. Retrain model with additional image samples if confusion occurs between similar species
12.2. Profile mobile CPU/GPU memory usage during TFLite inference using Android Studio Profiler

## 13. Usability Testing
13.1. Conduct field testing with non-technical users and fisherfolk
13.2. Evaluate ease of camera capture, clarity of confidence ratings, and visibility of geofence warnings
13.3. Adjust UI/UX based on direct feedback

